<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      ⚠️ **NOTHING THIS APPLICATION DRAWS COMES FROM THE NETWORK, and that is now a claim about the
      code rather than about this file.** The star catalogue, the constellations, the deep sky, the
      Milky Way and every planetary position are bundled or computed; put this phone in aeroplane
      mode and the map is complete. What INTERNET is for is the two things that are not the map:
      fetching a newer build of the app itself, and sending on a fault it has already recorded.

      ⚠️ **The previous version of this paragraph said "no INTERNET at all", and it was true when it
      was written.** It also said, in as many words, that the commit adding the self-updater would
      add these two permissions and rewrite it — because leaving that claim standing afterwards
      would be the overstated comment this repository treats as a defect. This is that rewrite.

      ⚠️ ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE is what lets the automatic update ask whether it is on Wi-Fi before it
      pulls tens of megabytes. Without it the question cannot be asked, and the honest fallback
      would be to never update automatically at all.

      ⚠️ **Two more are MERGED IN from `:core:update` and are not written here** —
      REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES and UPDATE_PACKAGES_WITHOUT_USER_ACTION. That library declares them
      once for every application that installs its own updates, which is the point of it; naming
      them again here would be a second statement of one fact. They are mentioned because a reader
      comparing this file against the installed app's permission list would otherwise find two it
      does not explain.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" />

    <!--
      ⚠️ **COARSE, never FINE, and the arithmetic is why.** A degree of latitude moves the sky by a
      degree, so a kilometre of error is under a hundredth of one — far below anything visible even
      at the quarter-degree field floor this map can zoom to. Android's coarse location is fuzzed to
      roughly a city block, which is three orders of magnitude better than the map can use. Asking
      for the precise location would be asking for something the feature has no use for.

      `SkyHardware.granted` accepts either, so a future FINE declaration would work without a code
      change — which is exactly why the decision has to be recorded here rather than left implied.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION" />

    <!--
      ⚠️ Every one `required="false"`, or this application would be hidden from phones that lack
      them — which is the opposite of the point of a universal APK. None of the three is needed to
      DRAW the sky: with no location the map says where it needs to be told, and with no
      rotation-vector sensor the FOLLOW control says the phone cannot follow. The stars are still
      all there, because they come from a file and some arithmetic.
    -->
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.location" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.location.gps" android:required="false" />
    <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.sensor.compass" android:required="false" />

    <application
        android:name=".SkyApplication"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
        android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Sky">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>
    </application>
</manifest>
